{"qid":"Q185047","names":["Spring and Autumn States"],"title":"Spring and Autumn States","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Spring and Autumn period (c. 770–481 BCE) marks the first half of the Eastern Zhou dynasty, during which the nominal Zhou king retained ritual prestige while real power fractured among roughly 170 competing states that gradually consolidated into a smaller number of powerful principalities. The era takes its name from the Spring and Autumn Annals, a chronicle of the state of Lu traditionally associated with Confucius. Interstate warfare, diplomatic congresses presided over by hegemonic lords (ba), and the first articulation of Confucian, Daoist, and legalist thought all emerged in this crucible. The period ended when the seven surviving major states discarded any remaining deference to Zhou suzerainty and entered the openly predatory Warring States era.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extents of individual Spring and Autumn states are reconstructed from textual sources and archaeology; boundaries are approximate and contested, particularly for peripheral or short-lived polities.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Spring and Autumn period","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_and_Autumn_period"},{"label":"Britannica: Spring and Autumn Period","url":"https://www.britannica.com/event/Spring-and-Autumn-Period"},{"label":"Britannica: Eastern Zhou dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Eastern-Zhou-dynasty"}],"britannica":"event/Spring-and-Autumn-Period","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Spring and Autumn Period","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"King Ping of Z